1://! Job logs — one participant's forensic record of one run (JOB_LOGS jl-1a).
2://!
3://! `docs/plan/JOB_LOGS.md` ("Log keys and event schema"; "Crash safety,
4://! pruning and backpressure") is the contract this module implements:
5://!
6://! - **Schema.** One JSON [`Event`] per line, each with a wall-clock
7://!   timestamp, a sequence number and a `kind`. The first event is
8://!   `run-start`, carrying [`FORMAT`] and [`VERSION`]. Readers skip fields
9://!   they do not know and read a kind they do not know as
10://!   [`EventBody::Unknown`], so adding a field or a kind is not a break; a
11://!   change an older reader would misread bumps [`VERSION`].
12://! - **Key.** A log is keyed by run ID + participant + role + attempt
13://!   ([`LogKey`]), so one daemon that plays two roles in one run writes two
14://!   logs, never one interleaved file.
15://! - **Writing.** [`LogWriter::start`] appends whole lines to
16://!   `<key>.partial.jsonl` from a thread of its own, fed through a bounded
17://!   queue: when the thread falls behind, the producer waits rather than
18://!   drop an event, because a log with holes misleads. The partial is synced
19://!   at every phase change and every few seconds. [`LogWriter::finish`]
20://!   writes `run-end`, compresses the partial to `<key>.jsonl.gz` through a
21://!   temp file and one rename, removes the partial, then prunes the folder.
22://! - **Ownership.** A writer holds an exclusive lock on a sidecar
23://!   `<key>.lock` for its whole life. The lock is on a separate file because
24://!   a Windows lock on the partial itself would stop a reader from serving
25://!   the active log.
26://! - **Crash.** [`recover`] (run at startup) finishes every partial whose
27://!   lock it can take — its writer is gone — with a `run-end` marked
28://!   interrupted. [`open_log`] tolerates the torn last line a crash leaves.
29://! - **Retention.** [`prune`] keeps the newest `keep` finished logs, under a
30://!   folder lock; it never touches a partial or any file that is not a log.
31://! - **Logging never fails a transfer** (R17). Nothing here returns an error
32://!   to the transfer: if the log cannot be written, logging stops, a
33://!   `log-incomplete` event is attempted, later events are dropped (so a
34://!   producer never waits on a dead log), and the [`LogReport`] says the log
35://!   is incomplete and why.
